    - id: f126b63999594e2c8816b44ad00ed74e
      blockGroup: f126b63999594e2c8816b44ad00ed74e
      type: text-cell-h2
      sortingKey: aw
      content: 6. Nulos
    - id: 82c805ff7fae4f969b5299beaed07977
      blockGroup: 82c805ff7fae4f969b5299beaed07977
      type: text-cell-p
      sortingKey: ax
      content: Un nulo es un dato ausente. COUNT(*) cuenta todas las filas y COUNT(columna) ignora los
        nulos. Para preguntar por nulos se usa IS NULL, nunca = NULL.
    - id: 9a56b3179af74837809aa2063f645b7e
      blockGroup: 9a56b3179af74837809aa2063f645b7e
      type: sql
      sortingKey: ay
      content: |
        SELECT
            COUNT(*) AS filas,
            COUNT(passenger_count) AS con_pasajeros,
            COUNT(*) - COUNT(passenger_count) AS sin_pasajeros
        FROM viajes
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
        deepnote_variable_name: df_nulos
        deepnote_return_variable_type: dataframe
    - id: 3bd88a61ce3d43c096e25359362a4cbc
      blockGroup: 3bd88a61ce3d43c096e25359362a4cbc
      type: code
      sortingKey: az
      content: |
        # El error clásico: comparar con = NULL no devuelve nada
        mal = consulta("SELECT COUNT(*) AS filas FROM viajes WHERE passenger_count = NULL")
        bien = consulta("SELECT COUNT(*) AS filas FROM viajes WHERE passenger_count IS NULL")
        print("= NULL   :", mal["filas"][0])    # siempre 0
        print("IS NULL  :", bien["filas"][0])
    - id: e0bbb6c8eb59488faea12859810d382e
      blockGroup: e0bbb6c8eb59488faea12859810d382e
      type: sql
      sortingKey: b0
      content: |
        SELECT
            passenger_count,
            COALESCE(passenger_count, 1) AS pasajeros_supuestos
        FROM viajes
        WHERE passenger_count IS NULL
        LIMIT 5
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
        deepnote_variable_name: df_coalesce
        deepnote_return_variable_type: dataframe
    - id: 399e4230550a4185a1f2c96994ffacfb
      blockGroup: 399e4230550a4185a1f2c96994ffacfb
      type: text-cell-h2
      sortingKey: b1
      content: 7. Práctica guiada
    - id: 1fb109a7913f49aab2aec4e9c428472e
      blockGroup: 1fb109a7913f49aab2aec4e9c428472e
      type: text-cell-p
      sortingKey: b2
      content: 'Juntamos lo anterior: viajes de más de 10 millas con propina mayor a 5 dólares, de la
        propina más alta a la más baja.'
    - id: 48dffb380fe542138a757a1a9228e971
      blockGroup: 48dffb380fe542138a757a1a9228e971
      type: sql
      sortingKey: b3
      content: |
        SELECT
            tpep_pickup_datetime,
            trip_distance,
            tip_amount,
            total_amount
        FROM viajes
        WHERE trip_distance > 10
          AND tip_amount > 5
        ORDER BY tip_amount DESC
        LIMIT 10
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
        deepnote_variable_name: df_largos_con_propina
        deepnote_return_variable_type: dataframe
    - id: 4af7f82e48574658ad01c242bca7c80b
      blockGroup: 4af7f82e48574658ad01c242bca7c80b
      type: text-cell-h2
      sortingKey: b4
      content: 8. INNER JOIN
    - id: 62e1f0b33382465380aee8c6bcc6940a
      blockGroup: 62e1f0b33382465380aee8c6bcc6940a
      type: text-cell-p
      sortingKey: b5
      content: Un join une dos tablas por una llave. Viajes es la tabla de hechos y zonas es la dimensión.
        INNER JOIN deja solo las filas que tienen pareja en las dos tablas.
    - id: 265d31295eb74e61ae8f78afda7f5c64
      blockGroup: 265d31295eb74e61ae8f78afda7f5c64
      type: sql
      sortingKey: b6
      content: |
        SELECT
            v.PULocationID,
            z.Zone AS zona_origen,
            z.Borough AS borough_origen,
            v.total_amount
        FROM viajes AS v
        INNER JOIN zonas AS z
            ON v.PULocationID = z.LocationID
        LIMIT 5
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
        deepnote_variable_name: df_join
        deepnote_return_variable_type: dataframe
    - id: 3d20b9a200874e9a8e1c9fbc91e741e6
      blockGroup: 3d20b9a200874e9a8e1c9fbc91e741e6
      type: sql
      sortingKey: b7
      content: |
        SELECT COUNT(*) AS filas_inner
        FROM viajes AS v
        INNER JOIN zonas AS z
            ON v.PULocationID = z.LocationID
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
        deepnote_variable_name: df_filas_inner
        deepnote_return_variable_type: dataframe
    - id: 6db9a0955dc141e4ad045ffa0699879c
      blockGroup: 6db9a0955dc141e4ad045ffa0699879c
      type: text-cell-h2
      sortingKey: b8
      content: 9. LEFT JOIN y filas huérfanas
    - id: c572fe428903482b8f1f7a772d109bc2
      blockGroup: c572fe428903482b8f1f7a772d109bc2
      type: text-cell-p
      sortingKey: b9
      content: LEFT JOIN conserva todas las filas de la izquierda y deja nulos donde no hay pareja. Una
        fila huérfana es un viaje cuya zona no existe en zonas. Para verlo con certeza, quitamos a propósito
        las zonas 264 y 265 de una copia (zonas_reducida). Con INNER JOIN esos viajes desaparecen sin
        avisar, con LEFT JOIN se quedan.
    - id: 53cf40c2812546b9af6e35b28cbe303e
      blockGroup: 53cf40c2812546b9af6e35b28cbe303e
      type: code
      sortingKey: ba
      content: |
        # Una copia de zonas SIN las zonas 264 y 265, para provocar filas huérfanas a propósito
        zonas_reducida = zonas.filter(~pl.col("LocationID").is_in([264, 265]))
        print(zonas.height, "->", zonas_reducida.height)
    - id: 20ed92624af1444aa7bd1fcbd466e362
      blockGroup: 20ed92624af1444aa7bd1fcbd466e362
      type: sql
      sortingKey: bb
      content: |
        SELECT 'INNER JOIN' AS tipo_join, COUNT(*) AS filas
        FROM viajes AS v
        INNER JOIN zonas_reducida AS z ON v.PULocationID = z.LocationID

        UNION ALL

        SELECT 'LEFT JOIN' AS tipo_join, COUNT(*) AS filas
        FROM viajes AS v
        LEFT JOIN zonas_reducida AS z ON v.PULocationID = z.LocationID
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
        deepnote_variable_name: df_comparacion_join
        deepnote_return_variable_type: dataframe
    - id: b15a76adfed14912afb36e8d2c7dd93a
      blockGroup: b15a76adfed14912afb36e8d2c7dd93a
      type: sql
      sortingKey: bc
      content: |
        SELECT
            v.PULocationID,
            COUNT(*) AS viajes
        FROM viajes AS v
        LEFT JOIN zonas_reducida AS z
            ON v.PULocationID = z.LocationID
        WHERE z.LocationID IS NULL
        GROUP BY v.PULocationID
        ORDER BY viajes DESC
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
        deepnote_variable_name: df_huerfanos
        deepnote_return_variable_type: dataframe
    - id: 3b5841049b544601b90ef2b12330a687
      blockGroup: 3b5841049b544601b90ef2b12330a687
      type: sql
      sortingKey: bd
      content: |
        SELECT
            z.LocationID,
            z.Borough,
            z.Zone,
            COUNT(*) AS viajes
        FROM viajes AS v
        INNER JOIN zonas AS z
            ON v.PULocationID = z.LocationID
        WHERE z.LocationID IN (264, 265)
        GROUP BY z.LocationID, z.Borough, z.Zone
        ORDER BY z.LocationID
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
        deepnote_variable_name: df_unknown
        deepnote_return_variable_type: dataframe
    - id: d05cf813869f4d7b98dfedeb70574f4f
      blockGroup: d05cf813869f4d7b98dfedeb70574f4f
      type: code
      sortingKey: be
      content: |
        # Control de calidad del join: contar antes y después. Si LEFT JOIN devuelve más filas
        # que viajes, la llave de zonas está repetida (fan-out).
        antes = viajes.height
        despues = consulta("""
            SELECT COUNT(*) AS filas
            FROM viajes AS v
            LEFT JOIN zonas AS z ON v.PULocationID = z.LocationID
        """)["filas"][0]
        print(f"Antes: {antes:,}   Después: {despues:,}   Iguales: {antes == despues}")
    - id: 0bba28f5762143f7b25943b4f98f3743
      blockGroup: 0bba28f5762143f7b25943b4f98f3743
      type: text-cell-h2
      sortingKey: bf
      content: 10. Funciones de agregación
    - id: 36d450e4d5504653baf81a6ed3dd77ab
      blockGroup: 36d450e4d5504653baf81a6ed3dd77ab
      type: text-cell-p
      sortingKey: bg
      content: 'COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN y MAX resumen muchas filas en un valor y ignoran los nulos. Observa
        el mínimo del resultado: si es negativo, ¿qué dice eso de la calidad de los datos?'
    - id: da9bea8250ef4ac8b0300def3a2a0a4f
      blockGroup: da9bea8250ef4ac8b0300def3a2a0a4f
      type: sql
      sortingKey: bh
      content: |
        SELECT
            COUNT(*) AS viajes,
            ROUND(SUM(total_amount), 2) AS ingreso_total,
            ROUND(AVG(total_amount), 2) AS ticket_promedio,
            MIN(total_amount) AS minimo,
            MAX(total_amount) AS maximo
        FROM viajes
      metadata:
        sql_integration_id: deepnote-dataframe-sql
